---
import Prose from "../layouts/Prose.astro";
import { publisher, repo } from "../lib/site";
---

<Prose title="About" active="about" description="Who runs charityevals, why it exists and how to contribute.">
  <p>
    charityevals publishes cost-effectiveness analyses of charities as open, readable models. Each shows the numbers it uses and where they come from, and lets you change the value judgments that decide how charities compare.
  </p>

  <h2>Why</h2>
  <p>
    Charity evaluators publish excellent analyses, mostly as large spreadsheets that are hard to read and harder to compare across causes. Many charities, including some of the best known, such as the Salvation Army and the Red Cross, have none at all. This site rebuilds published analyses so they can be read, compared and challenged, and builds its own where no evaluator has looked.
  </p>

  <h2>Who</h2>
  <p>
    charityevals is an independent project run by <a href={publisher.url}>{publisher.name}</a> in {publisher.city}. It is not affiliated with GiveWell, Animal Charity Evaluators or any charity on the site, and receives no money from them. Where a model is based on an evaluator's work, its page links the source and shows where we differ.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
  <p>
    Every model is a file in a <a href={repo}>public repository</a>, and every change is visible in its history. If a number is wrong, open an issue or a pull request with a source, or email <a href={`mailto:${publisher.email}`}>{publisher.email}</a>. Corrections with a source are welcome from anyone, including the charities themselves. The postal address is in the <a href="/legal">legal notice</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
